; SW-58 — which handler answers when a self tail call sits in a `guard` body.
;
; The native engines lower that call as a loop back edge; the VM compiles a
; guard body with tail=0 and keeps one activation per level. The two engines
; must still agree on WHICH of the live guards answers and with WHOSE
; variables, because that is a property of the program, not of the engine.
; Depths are kept small: the VM's handler stack holds 16 frames.

; 1. innermost handler re-raises: the answer names the next guard out
(define (s n)
  (guard (e ((= n 0) (raise 'again)) (#t (list 'inner n)))
    (if (= n 0) (raise 'boom) (s (- n 1)))))
(display (guard (e (#t 'outer)) (s 3))) (newline)

; 2. every level re-raises but the outermost, which answers with its own n
(define (climb n)
  (guard (e ((> n 0) (raise e)) (#t (list 'answered-at n)))
    (if (>= n 5) (raise 'top) (climb (+ n 1)))))
(display (climb 0)) (newline)

; 3. a raise from inside a CLAUSE lands on the previous activation's guard,
;    and each level rewrites the payload, so the value names the whole chain
(define (relay n)
  (guard (e ((> n 0) (raise (cons n e))) (#t (list 'bottom e)))
    (if (>= n 3) (raise 'boom) (relay (+ n 1)))))
(display (relay 0)) (newline)

; 4. a catch-all boundary still catches a raise from a later iteration, with
;    that iteration's own accumulator
(define (stumble i acc)
  (guard (e (#t (list 'caught-at i acc)))
    (if (= i 200) (raise 'stumble) (stumble (+ i 1) (+ acc 2)))))
(display (stumble 0 0)) (newline)
